body:
LLRT should support module:registerHooks() to achieve the following use cases:

- When you want to quickly create a completely new module.
- When you want to add missing functionality to an existing native module.

We expect the following implementation to work:

```javascript
// register-hook.js
import { registerHooks } from "node:module";

registerHooks({
  resolve(specifier, context, nextResolve) {
    if (specifier === "fs" || specifier === "node:fs") {
      return {
        url: "llrt-polyfill:fs",
        format: "module",
        shortCircuit: true,
      };
    } else if (specifier.startsWith("internal:")) {
      specifier = specifier.replace("internal:", "");
    }
    return nextResolve(specifier, context);
  },
  load(url, context, nextLoad) {
    if (url === "llrt-polyfill:fs") {
      const code = `
        export * from "internal:fs";
        import fs from "internal:fs";
        export function existsSync(path) {
          try {
            fs.accessSync(path);
            return true;
          } catch {
            return false;
          }
        }
      `;

      return { format: "module", shortCircuit: true, source: code };
    }
    return nextLoad(url, context);
  },
});
```
```javascript
// reproduction.js
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
console.log(existsSync("./register-hook.js"));
```

```
% llrt --import register-hook.js reproduction.js
true
```

~NOTE: The basic specifications follow `module.registerHooks`([url](https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#moduleregisterhooksoptions)), but we think it's better to specify the source for the hook using environment variables rather than the command line. Because it also needs to be taken into account that it will be executed by Lambda.~

