@perpil I now know what causes this and is quite a tricky problem.
LLRT supports seamless CJS and MJS modules. Very simplified require works buy calling an asynchronous import and looping to that inport is resolved. However, for async tasks inside CJS modules at top level the runtime (and the require function) can't advance the asynchronous runtime and scheduler inside rquickjs. This mechanism is not exposed. We call `require()` on the handler which effectively treats it as a CJS module this TLA will not work.

The workaround for this is for us to always treat the handler as MJS (which allows for TLS). Using a CJS handler will still work but will freeze the runtime. There might be a way for us to detect if TLA is defined in a CJS module but I'm not 100% sure yet.

