#### Describe the bug
When using the `DynamoDBDocumentClient` from `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb` to perform a `Put` or `TransactWrite` operation, an error `Special numeric value 0 is not allowed` is thrown if the `Item` object contains a nested map where one of the values is the number `0`.

This issue seems to stem from the internal marshalling process, which incorrectly handles the numeric value `0` within a nested object, leading to an unexpected error. The operation works as expected if the values are non-zero.

#### To Reproduce
The following code demonstrates the issue within a Lambda environment. The error occurs when trying to put an item with a nested object `imageOffset: { x: 0, y: 0 }`.

```javascript
import { DynamoDBClient } from "@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb";
import { DynamoDBDocumentClient, TransactWriteCommand } from "@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb";

// Standard client initialization that causes the error
const client = new DynamoDBClient({ region: "us-east-1" });
const docClient = DynamoDBDocumentClient.from(client);

export const handler = async () => {
  const params = {
    TransactItems: [
      {
        Put: {
          TableName: "YourTableName",
          Item: {
            id: "item-123",
            ownerId: "user-abc",
            // This nested object with '0' values triggers the error
            imageOffset: {
              x: 0,
              y: 0
            },
            version: 1,
          },
        },
      },
    ],
  };

  try {
    const command = new TransactWriteCommand(params);
    await docClient.send(command);
    console.log("Transaction succeeded.");
  } catch (error) {
    // The following error is thrown
    console.error("Handler error:", error);
    throw error;
  }
};```

